    "datePosted": "2026-11-10T11:47:34.000Z",
    "description": "Vacancy Announcement MSD is an indigenous, non-profit civil society organization established in 1999 with a mission to improve the health, well-being, and socio-economic conditions of communities across Ethiopia. Over the past decades, MSD has evolved into a well-established development and humanitarian organization, delivering evidence-based, community-centered programs that respond to the evolving needs of vulnerable and underserved populations. MSD implements integrated programs across a broad range of thematic areas, including: Health and Nutrition: TB, HIV/AIDS, sexual and reproductive health (SRH), maternal, newborn and child health (MNCH), and nutrition. Education and Skills Development: Access to quality education, technical and vocational education and training (TVET), and skills development. Social Mobilization and Livelihoods: Economic strengthening, community mobilization, and prevention of harmful traditional practices (HTPs), gender-based violence (GBV), and female genital mutilation (FGM), Migration and Trafficking: Safe migration, protection, rehabilitation, and reintegration support for migrants, returnees, and victims of trafficking; Child Care and Development: Child protection, early childhood care and development, and infant and young child feeding. , Human Rights and Good Governance: Democratization, peace building, social accountability, civic engagement, and dialogue; Humanitarian and Emergency Response: Integrated assistance and protection services for communities affected by conflict, displacement, disasters, and other emergencies; and Environment and Climate Change: Environmental sustainability, climate resilience, and biodiversity conservation. Through these integrated programs, MSD works in close collaboration with federal and regional government institutions, communities, civil society organizations, development and humanitarian partners, and other stakeholders to strengthen local systems, expand access to essential services, promote inclusion and equity, and build resilient communities across Ethiopia. Project Background: The Border Security Programme (BSP) is a UK Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO)-funded regional initiative designed to address organized immigration crime (OIC), strengthen border security, and contribute to safer borders and communities. The programme seeks to reduce the activities and influence of criminal networks involved in facilitating irregular migration, human smuggling, human trafficking, document fraud, and other forms of organized immigration-related crime. It adopts a comprehensive, multi-sectoral approach that combines institutional capacity strengthening, evidence-based decision-making, community participation, and protection support for individuals affected by organized immigration crime. MSD is looking for qualified and competent applicants for the following position: Position: Refferal Corridor Officer Location: Gondar Salary: Negotiable subject to organizational salary scale and project budget No. Required: 1 Gender: Both male and female are invited, and women are highly encouraged to apply. Term of Employment: Contract Job Summary The Referral Corridor Officer is responsible for coordinating, strengthening, and maintaining effective protection referral pathways along the Northern Migration Corridor, with a particular focus on facilitating access to essential services for vulnerable migrants, returnees, unaccompanied and separated children (UASC), and survivors of human trafficking. Based in Gondar and reporting to the Migration/Protection Coordinator, the Officer ensures effective coordination and follow-up of referrals between relevant government institutions, humanitarian organizations, civil society organizations, and specialized service providers. The position supports the identification and mapping of available services, establishment and strengthening of referral networks, coordination of cross-location and cross-border referral arrangements, and monitoring of referral outcomes to ensure timely, safe, accessible, and appropriate assistance. The Officer works closely with Migration/Protection Officers, Referral Officers, GBV Case Management Officers, government counterparts, and other programme stakeholders to strengthen continuity of care, protection services, and reintegration support. The role also ensures proper referral documentation, confidentiality, informed consent, data protection, and compliance with survivor-centred and do-no-harm principles. 1. Key Duties and Responsibilities:- Referral Pathway Coordination: Establish, strengthen, and maintain functional referral pathways among origin, transit, and destination locations to ensure vulnerable migrants and trafficking survivors can access timely and appropriate protection services. Identification and Referral: Facilitate the identification, initial screening, and safe referral of vulnerable migrants, trafficking survivors, UASC, and individuals exposed to exploitation, abuse, or other protection risks. Stakeholder Coordination: Maintain effective working relationships with immigration authorities, police, justice offices, social affairs institutions, shelters, health facilities, NGOs, and other relevant service providers along migration corridors. Case Follow-up and Continuity of Care: Coordinate and follow up on referrals to ensure individuals receive appropriate services, including medical care, psychosocial support, legal assistance, shelter, family tracing, reunification, and reintegration support. Cross-Regional and Cross-Border Coordination: Facilitate coordination and information sharing among relevant stakeholders across migration corridors and administrative boundaries, ensuring safe continuity of protection services. Referral Mapping and Service Provider Database: Identify and regularly update information on available protection services, shelters, health facilities, legal assistance providers, and other referral institutions along designated migration routes. Protection Monitoring and Risk Identification: Monitor emerging migration-related protection risks, trafficking trends, exploitation patterns, and service delivery gaps along referral corridors, and communicate findings to programme management. Capacity Building and Technical Support: Support government institutions, community structures, and service providers to strengthen their understanding of safe migration, human trafficking, identification of vulnerable migrants, protection principles, and referral procedures. Documentation and Reporting: Maintain appropriate referral records, track referral outcomes, document service gaps, and prepare regular activity and progress reports while ensuring confidentiality and protection of personal information. Safeguarding and Accountability: Ensure all referral processes adhere to informed consent, confidentiality, data protection, survivor-centered approaches, child safeguarding, PSEA, and the Do No Harm principle.",
